# ── Server ──────────────────────────────────────────────
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=5000
API_PREFIX=/api/v1

# ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────
MONGO_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/mysteryrooms_erp

# ── Auth ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Use long random strings in production (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 48`)
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=change-me-access-secret-in-production
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=change-me-refresh-secret-in-production
JWT_ACCESS_EXPIRES_IN=15m
JWT_REFRESH_EXPIRES_IN=7d

# ── CORS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated list of allowed origins. Matched against the browser's
# Origin header: scheme + host, no path. A trailing slash is tolerated and
# stripped, since pasting from the address bar is how this usually goes wrong.
# In production this is the Netlify site, e.g.
#   CLIENT_ORIGINS=https://mysteryrooms.netlify.app
#
# `*` accepts ANY origin. It does not emit a literal `*` header (illegal on a
# credentialed request, and every request here carries the refresh cookie) —
# it reflects the caller's own Origin instead. Use it to get a deployment
# unblocked, not as the resting state: with the SameSite=None refresh cookie,
# any site a signed-in user visits can then call this API as them. The server
# warns on every boot while it is set.
CLIENT_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# ── Refresh-cookie attributes ───────────────────────────
# Leave both unset — they are derived from NODE_ENV, which is correct for the
# two deployments that exist: Lax over http in dev, None+Secure in production
# (Netlify frontend + Render backend are different sites, and a Lax cookie is
# not sent on a cross-site POST /auth/refresh — the session would drop at the
# first token refresh).
# Set them only if you later serve the API from the SPA's own domain:
#   COOKIE_SAMESITE=lax
#   COOKIE_SECURE=true

# ── Cloudinary (file/image uploads) ─────────────────────
# From your Cloudinary dashboard (https://cloudinary.com/console).
# All three are required to enable uploads; leave blank to disable.
CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=

# ── AI module (property / location intelligence) ────────
# Set at least one provider key to switch the module on. With none set the
# endpoints answer 503 and the UI shows a "not configured" state — the server
# still boots normally.
#
# Switching vendor is an env change only. AI_PROVIDER takes "auto" or an
# explicit comma-separated order, which is also the failover chain:
#
#   auto          → every key that is set, in preference order
#                   (gemini, grok, groq, openai)
#   groq          → Groq only
#   groq,gemini   → Groq first, fall over to Gemini if it fails
#   gemini,openai → the other way round
#
# NOTE: `grok` and `groq` are different vendors, one letter apart —
#   grok = xAI's models      (api.x.ai,     keys start "xai-")
#   groq = an inference host (api.groq.com, keys start "gsk_")
# Neither falls back to the other; a key in the wrong slot fails with a 401.
#
# An unknown name here stops the server at boot with a list of valid ones.
# Verify a key and its model with:  node check-ai-key.mjs [grok|groq|gemini|openai]
AI_ENABLED=true
AI_PROVIDER=auto

# ── xAI / Grok — https://console.x.ai ──
# GROK_API_KEY is accepted as an alias for XAI_API_KEY.
XAI_API_KEY=
XAI_MODEL=grok-4-fast-reasoning
XAI_BASE_URL=https://api.x.ai/v1
# Live Search is a metered add-on and is what produces the report's citations.
#   auto → search when useful; if the account has no search entitlement the run
#          continues ungrounded and the report is labelled as unverified
#   on   → grounding required; a run that cannot search fails (and fails over)
#   off  → never search, never pay for it
XAI_SEARCH_MODE=auto
XAI_MAX_SEARCH_RESULTS=15

# ── Groq — https://console.groq.com/keys ──
# The one provider that splits the pipeline across two models, because on Groq
# grounding and strict JSON output live on different ones:
#   GROQ_RESEARCH_MODEL  agentic, runs server-side web search → citations
#   GROQ_MODEL           plain reasoning model → strict JSON synthesis
# Using the search-capable model for both costs ~20x the tokens (it inlines
# every fetched page), which the free tier cannot fit. See groq.provider.js.
#
# Free tier is 8,000 tokens/minute — enough for roughly one analysis at a time.
# Runs that 429 are retried, but for concurrent use raise the tier or set a
# second provider as failover (AI_PROVIDER=groq,gemini).
GROQ_API_KEY=
GROQ_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-120b
GROQ_RESEARCH_MODEL=groq/compound
GROQ_BASE_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1
# auto → search when useful, continue ungrounded (and say so) if unavailable
# on   → grounding required; a run that cannot search fails and fails over
# off  → never search
GROQ_SEARCH_MODE=auto
# Bounds how many citations are kept (Groq offers no way to cap what is
# fetched without pushing the request over its size limit).
GROQ_MAX_SEARCH_RESULTS=10
# Groq rejects (413) any request whose completion budget exceeds what is left
# in the token/minute window, instead of truncating. The analysis services ask
# for up to 16k, sized for providers without that limit, so it is clamped here.
# Raise this with your Groq tier; 0 disables clamping.
GROQ_MAX_COMPLETION_TOKENS=6000

# ── OpenAI — https://platform.openai.com/api-keys ──
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
# Two models, as with Groq: synthesis is judgement over an assembled brief,
# research is a tool-using web crawl. Measured on this pipeline's own prompts:
# the top tier blew the 120s timeout with web_search attached and returned a
# gateway 520, so it is not viable for research whatever its quality.
OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5.4-mini
OPENAI_RESEARCH_MODEL=gpt-5.4-mini
OPENAI_WEB_SEARCH_TOOL=web_search
# `on` requires the research call to actually search and come back with
# citations, failing the run otherwise. The permissive `auto` was observed
# returning a confident, uncited brief written from the model's priors — which
# then scores a multi-year lease. `off` never searches.
OPENAI_SEARCH_MODE=on
# Dominant lever on synthesis latency: on an identical brief this swung the
# call between 38s (low) and 106s (model default). Reasoning models only.
OPENAI_REASONING_EFFORT=low

# ── Google Gemini — https://aistudio.google.com/apikey ──
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-pro
GEMINI_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta

# A grounded research call + a synthesis call routinely takes 30–90s.
AI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
AI_MAX_RETRIES=2
# Hours a completed analysis stays authoritative before a re-run is suggested.
AI_CACHE_TTL_HOURS=168
# A `running` analysis older than this is presumed dead and swept to `failed`.
AI_RUN_STALE_MINUTES=15
# Properties analysed at once by a whole-project sweep. Each is several provider
# calls, so this multiplies into concurrent vendor load — raise it only with
# your provider's requests-per-minute allowance.
AI_BULK_CONCURRENCY=3

# ── Rate limiting ───────────────────────────────────────
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=900000
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=300
# AI calls cost money, so they get a tighter budget of their own.
AI_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=3600000
AI_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=60

# ── Logging ─────────────────────────────────────────────
LOG_LEVEL=debug
LOG_DIR=logs

# ── Email (activity reminders) ──────────────────────────
# Optional. With SMTP_HOST unset the app runs normally and reminders are
# delivered in-app only; the mailer logs one warning and skips every send.
# The recipient is never configured here — it is read from the owner's own
# account, so an address changed in the employee record changes where the
# reminder goes.
#
# Gmail needs an App Password (not the account password) with 2FA enabled:
#   SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com  SMTP_PORT=587  SMTP_USER=you@gmail.com
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
# Leave unset: it is inferred as true for port 465, false otherwise.
SMTP_SECURE=
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
MAIL_FROM=Mystery Rooms ERP <no-reply@mysteryrooms.in>

# Where the "Open in CRM" button in a reminder email points. The app's own
# address, not the API's — a recipient clicking it wants the record, not JSON.
APP_URL=http://localhost:5173

# ── CRM lead capture ────────────────────────────────────
# Per-form secrets for the PUBLIC intake endpoint, as form:secret pairs.
# One key per form on purpose — these sit in public HTML, so assume each one
# leaks eventually, and revoking a leaked key must not take the other forms
# down with it. The server decides `formId` from the key it recognises, so a
# caller cannot claim to be a different form.
# Unset = the public endpoint refuses everything, which is the right default.
#   curl -H "X-Form-Key: <secret>" -d '{"name":"..","phone":".."}' \
#        http://localhost:5000/api/v1/crm/public/leads
CRM_FORM_KEYS=

# Meta Lead Ads (Facebook + Instagram). All three secrets are needed together:
# a webhook that verifies but cannot fetch the lead data produces empty leads.
# The page token MUST be long-lived and carry `leads_retrieval` — a short-lived
# one expires in about an hour and lead capture then dies silently.
META_VERIFY_TOKEN=
META_APP_SECRET=
META_PAGE_TOKEN=
META_GRAPH_VERSION=v21.0

# ── Telephony (Exotel) ──────────────────────────────────
# Optional. Unset = click-to-call is refused with a clear message and inbound
# webhooks are rejected; nothing else in the CRM changes.
#
# The recording-consent announcement is configured ON THE EXOTEL FLOW, not
# here — see docs/CRM-COMMS-ONBOARDING.md. It is a legal requirement in India
# and must be set at number provisioning, not retro-fitted.
TELEPHONY_PROVIDER=exotel
TELEPHONY_SID=
TELEPHONY_TOKEN=
# The number the CUSTOMER sees. Masking is why this is not a tel: link.
TELEPHONY_CALLER_ID=
# Exotel does not sign webhooks, so the callback URL carries this instead.
# Make it long and random — anyone who learns it can post to the webhook.
TELEPHONY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Account-specific API host, given at provisioning (e.g. api.in.exotel.com).
TELEPHONY_SUBDOMAIN=api.exotel.com
